==============================================================
=/                      V P O R T                           \=
==============================================================

      A tiny, deterministic display protocol and server
         for embedded and point-of-sale Linux systems


--[ 1. WHAT IS VPORT ]----------------------------------------

VPort is a minimal graphics protocol for Linux. A client (a
script, a point-of-sale program, a kiosk application) talks to
a display server through a Unix domain socket or, across the
network, through an authenticated and encrypted channel, using
a small binary wire protocol. The server draws what the client
asks for in an isolated "portal" (viewport).

VPort is the PROTOCOL. It is defined by a specification, and
not by any piece of software. This source tree contains the
specification and one reference implementation of each side,
but anyone may write another client library or another server,
in any language. If it follows the specification, it works
with every other conforming piece.

VPort is NOT a replacement for X11 or Wayland on the desktop.
It does not run browsers, IDEs or toolkits. It exists for
machines that show a handful of well defined screens: cash
registers, self-service totems, information panels, embedded
controllers and terminal scripts.


--[ 2. DESIGN GOALS ]-----------------------------------------

  Small      The whole graphical stack should fit in less than
             20 MB of RAM. An X11 + Chromium kiosk setup
             typically needs 400 MB to 1 GB per terminal.

  Isolated   Every client owns its portals and can see nothing
             else. There is no screen scraping and no global
             key grabbing. Clients are identified by the
             kernel (SO_PEERCRED) on the local socket and by a
             certificate on the network, never by what they
             claim.

  Resilient  Application logic (client) and rendering (server)
             are strictly separated. The server must be able
             to restart in milliseconds without taking the
             application down.

  Simple     Binary commands, one thread, one epoll loop, a
             handful of dependencies, hard limits on
             everything a client can allocate.

  Networked  A server and its clients can run on different
             machines, over a mutually authenticated and
             encrypted channel. There is no plaintext network
             mode.


--[ 3. NON-GOALS ]--------------------------------------------

  - Running generic desktop applications.
  - Window management, decorations, compositing effects.
  - Rendering HTML, CSS or any markup.
  - Entering PINs or card data, which needs certified
    hardware.


--[ 4. THE PARTS ]--------------------------------------------

  VPort            The protocol. Specified in protocol/PROTOCOL.
                   protocol/vport-proto holds the reference wire
                   definitions in Rust, used by the programs below.
                   Nothing forces an implementation to use them:
                   the specification is enough.

  libvport         A client library. It connects to a server and
                   exposes calls such as init_portal, draw_rect,
                   show_text and commit. This one is the reference,
                   written in Rust, with a C ABI planned for C,
                   Python and shell bindings. Anyone can write
                   another one.

  vportd           A server. It listens on a Unix socket, validates
                   every message, keeps the state of each portal,
                   presents committed frames and routes input. This
                   one is both the default server and the reference
                   implementation. Anyone can write another one.

  vport-gateway    A gateway for network clients. It accepts
                   TLS 1.3 connections with mutual authentication,
                   lets in only the identities on an allow list,
                   and relays each client to vportd through a
                   socket reserved for gateways. It is optional:
                   vportd works alone for local use.

  libvport, vportd and vport-gateway do not depend on each
  other. They only share the protocol.


--[ 5. BUILDING ]---------------------------------------------

  A recent stable Rust toolchain is the only requirement.

      $ cargo build --release
      $ cargo test

  The SDL2 backend of vportd is optional and needs the SDL2
  development package:

      $ cargo build --release -p vportd --features sdl2

  The KMS backend draws straight to the screen from a Linux
  virtual console and needs no extra package:

      $ cargo build --release -p vportd --features kms

  Text needs a TrueType font on the machine running vportd.
  It looks under /usr/share/fonts, preferring DejaVu Sans,
  Noto Sans and Liberation Sans.

  The script util/pki.sh needs openssl.


--[ 6. RUNNING ]----------------------------------------------

  The default socket is /run/vport/vport.sock, which needs
  root. For development use a socket in /tmp:

      $ export VPORT_SOCKET=/tmp/vport-dev.sock
      $ cargo run -p vportd --features sdl2

  and in another terminal:

      $ export VPORT_SOCKET=/tmp/vport-dev.sock
      $ cargo run -p libvport --example demo

  The demo draws a product screen with the VPort logo, grabs
  the keyboard and shows the digits you type, the same way a
  barcode scanner would type them. Enter prints the code in
  the terminal and a click on the green button closes the
  demo.

  Closing the window or pressing Escape stops vportd.

  Optional environment for vportd:

      VPORT_BACKEND          "ppm" or "sdl2". Default is
                             sdl2 when built with the
                             sdl2 feature, else ppm
      VPORT_PPM              file written by the ppm
                             backend (default
                             /tmp/vport-frame.ppm)
      VPORT_SIZE             screen size as WIDTHxHEIGHT
                             (default 800x480)
      VPORT_FONT             TrueType font used to draw
                             text (default: found under
                             /usr/share/fonts)
      VPORT_GRAB_UIDS        comma separated uids allowed
                             to grab the keyboard (default:
                             0 and the uid of vportd)
      VPORT_GRAB_IDENTITIES  comma separated network
                             identities allowed to grab
                             the keyboard (default: none)
      VPORT_GATEWAY_SOCKET   socket reserved for the
                             gateway (default: none, so no
                             gateway can connect)
      VPORT_GATEWAY_UIDS     comma separated uids trusted
                             as gateway (default: 0 and
                             the uid of vportd)
      VPORT_DRM_DEVICE       graphics device used by the kms
                             backend (default: the first
                             /dev/dri/card* with a connected
                             display)
      VPORT_EVDEV_GRAB       "1" makes the kms backend take
                             the keyboards exclusively, so
                             that nothing else on the machine
                             receives their keys (default: 0).
                             Ctrl+Alt+Backspace stops vportd


--[ 7. RUNNING OVER THE NETWORK ]-----------------------------

  Network clients connect to a vport-gateway that runs next
  to vportd. Create a certificate authority, a certificate
  for the gateway and one for each client. The Common Name of
  a client certificate is its identity:

      $ sh util/pki.sh init
      $ sh util/pki.sh server gateway 127.0.0.1
      $ sh util/pki.sh client app01
      $ echo app01 > pki/allow.txt

  Start vportd with a socket reserved for the gateway:

      $ VPORT_SOCKET=/tmp/vport-dev.sock \
        VPORT_GATEWAY_SOCKET=/tmp/vport-gw.sock \
        cargo run -p vportd --features sdl2

  and the gateway:

      $ VPORT_GATEWAY_LISTEN=127.0.0.1:7443 \
        VPORT_GATEWAY_CERT=pki/gateway.crt \
        VPORT_GATEWAY_KEY=pki/gateway.key \
        VPORT_GATEWAY_CA=pki/ca.crt \
        VPORT_GATEWAY_ALLOW=pki/allow.txt \
        VPORT_GATEWAY_UPSTREAM=/tmp/vport-gw.sock \
        cargo run -p vport-gateway

  Environment for vport-gateway. All are required except the
  last one:

      VPORT_GATEWAY_LISTEN    address to listen on. There is
                              no default: the gateway never
                              listens unless told to
      VPORT_GATEWAY_CERT      certificate of the gateway
      VPORT_GATEWAY_KEY       private key of the gateway,
                              not readable by group or
                              others
      VPORT_GATEWAY_CA        authority that signs the
                              client certificates
      VPORT_GATEWAY_ALLOW     file with the allowed
                              identities, one per line
      VPORT_GATEWAY_UPSTREAM  gateway socket of vportd
                              (default
                              /run/vport/gateway.sock)

  The allow list is read again for every new connection.
  Network clients cannot grab the keyboard unless their
  identity is listed in VPORT_GRAB_IDENTITIES.

  libvport does not speak TLS yet. Until it does, a client
  machine can bridge a local socket to the gateway with
  socat:

      $ BRIDGE=OPENSSL:127.0.0.1:7443
      $ BRIDGE=$BRIDGE,cert=pki/app01.crt,key=pki/app01.key
      $ BRIDGE=$BRIDGE,cafile=pki/ca.crt
      $ socat UNIX-LISTEN:/tmp/vport-remote.sock,fork $BRIDGE

  and run the demo with VPORT_SOCKET=/tmp/vport-remote.sock.
  The bridge leaves its socket open to every local process of
  that machine, so it is only for testing.

  Keep pki/ca.key offline, because whoever has it can create
  any identity. The pki directory is ignored by git and must
  never be committed.


--[ 8. STATUS ]-----------------------------------------------

  VPort is in early development and the protocol is still a
  draft. Working today: wire protocol v0, a server with PPM
  and SDL2 backends, portals with clipped and alpha blended
  rectangles, images and text, keyboard and pointer button
  input, focus, the exclusive keyboard grab, a gateway for
  network clients over mutually authenticated TLS 1.3, and
  the reference client library. Planned next:

      - TLS inside libvport
      - formal specification with MUST/SHOULD wording and
        byte exact test vectors
      - conformance tests for servers and client libraries
      - limits per client address in the gateway
      - closing a connection when its identity leaves the
        allow list
      - fuzzing of the frame parser and an independent
        security review
      - MeasureText, to measure a text before drawing it
      - more pixel formats, such as RGB565
      - DRM/KMS backend with evdev input and EVIOCGRAB
      - PointerMove events
      - secure attention key
      - scene retention and reconnection in libvport
      - C ABI for libvport


